{#ch06-0001}
# The Song of Hárbarðr

{#ch06-0002}
Þórr travelled from the east-way[^9] and came to a sound. On the other side of the sound was the ferryman with the boat.[^10]

{#ch06-0003}
::: verse
Þórr called out:
:::

{#ch06-0004 ref="hrbl.1"}
::: stanza
‘Who’s that boy of boys[^11] who stands on the other side of the sound?’
He replied:
:::

{#ch06-0005 ref="hrbl.2"}
::: stanza
‘Who’s that old man of old men who shouts across the strait?’[^12]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0006 ref="hrbl.3"}
::: stanza
‘Ferry me over the sound, I’ll feed you in the morning\![^13]
I’ve a basket on my back, the food couldn’t be better;
I ate at leisure, before I left home,[^14]
herrings and he-goats[^15] — I’m still stuffed with it!’
The ferryman said:
:::

{#ch06-0007 ref="hrbl.4"}
::: stanza
‘You boast of early deeds at breakfast,[^16]
you don’t fully understand what lies ahead:
your household’s downcast, I think your mother’s dead!’[^17]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0008 ref="hrbl.5"}
::: stanza
‘Now you’re saying that which will seem to everyone
most distressing, that my mother is dead.’
The ferryman said:
:::

{#ch06-0009 ref="hrbl.6"}
::: stanza
‘It’s not as if you own three good farms;[^18]
you stand barelegged and have a beggar’s garb —
you don’t even have your breeches!’[^19]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0010 ref="hrbl.7"}
::: stanza
‘Steer the little ferryboat over here! I’ll show you the landing-place —
and who owns the ship which you keep by the shore?’
The ferryman said:
:::

{#ch06-0011 ref="hrbl.8"}
::: stanza
‘Hildólfr [^20] he’s called, who asked me to keep it,
the counsel-shrewd warrior who lives in Ráðseyjarsund;[^21]
he told me not to ferry robbers or rustlers of horses,
only good men and those whom I know well;
state your name, if you want to travel across the sound!’[^22]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0012 ref="hrbl.9"}
::: stanza
‘I would tell my name, even if I were outlawed,
and all my lineage, too: I am Óðinn’s son,
Meili’s[^23] brother and Magni’s[^24] father,
strong-ruler of gods — it’s Þórr you’re speaking to here\!
Now I want to ask this, what you are called.’
The ferryman said:
:::

{#ch06-0013 ref="hrbl.10"}
::: stanza
‘I’m called Hárbarðr, I seldom hide my name!’[^25]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0014 ref="hrbl.11"}
::: stanza
‘Why should you hide your name, unless you have disputes?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0015 ref="hrbl.12"}
::: stanza
‘But even if I have disputes, I’d defend my life
before such a one as you are, unless I were doomed!’[^26]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0016 ref="hrbl.13"}
::: stanza
‘A horrible shame it seems to me in this respect,
to wade over the bay to you and wet my burden(?)\![^27]
I’d pay you back for your insolent words,
you swaddling-clothed(?) brat,[^28] if I could get over the sound!’[^29]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0017 ref="hrbl.14"}
::: stanza
‘Here I’ll stand and here I’ll await you;
you’ve not found a harder man since Hrungnir’s death!’[^30]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0018 ref="hrbl.15"}
::: stanza
‘Now you want to talk of the time when Hrungnir and I had dealings,
that stout-hearted giant whose head was made of stone;
even so, I made him fall and sink before me\!
What were you doing meanwhile, Hárbarðr?’[^31]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0019 ref="hrbl.16"}
::: stanza
‘I was with Fjǫlvarr all of five years,
on the island which is called Algrœn;
we fought there and felled warriors,
tested many things, tried out the girls!’[^32]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0020 ref="hrbl.17"}
::: stanza
‘How did it turn out with your women?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0021 ref="hrbl.18"}
::: stanza
‘We had sparky women, if they submitted to us;[^33]
we had knowing women, if they were nice to us;[^34]
out of sand they plaited ropes
and out of a deep dale
they dug the ground;
I alone became superior to them[^35] all by scheming,
I slept beside those seven sisters,
and I had all their lust and love-play\![^36]
What were you doing meanwhile, Þórr?’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0022 ref="hrbl.19"}
::: stanza
‘I slew Þjazi, the strong-minded giant,[^37]
I threw the eyes of Allvaldi’s son[^38] up
into the shining sky;[^39]
they are the greatest signs of my deeds,
those which all people since may see\!
What were you doing meanwhile, Hárbarðr?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0023 ref="hrbl.20"}
::: stanza
‘Mighty girl-wiles[^40] I had for mirk-riders,[^41]
when I lured them away from men\![^42]
A hard giant I thought Hlébarðr [^43] to be;
he gave me a tribute(?)-twig,[^44]
and I wangled him out of his wits!’[^45]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0024 ref="hrbl.21"}
::: stanza
‘With ill-will you repaid good gifts, then.’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0025 ref="hrbl.22"}
::: stanza
‘\[One\] oak has what’s scraped from another;[^46]
it’s everyone for himself in such things\!
What were you doing meanwhile, Þórr?’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0026 ref="hrbl.23"}
::: stanza
‘I was in the east and I fought giants,
brides skilled in mischief who went to a mountain;[^47]
the kindred of giants would be large if all had lived —
there would be no men under Miðgarðr\![^48]
What were you doing meanwhile, Hárbarðr?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0027 ref="hrbl.24"}
::: stanza
‘I was in Valland [^49] and I followed[^50] battles;
I incited princes, and I never made peace;[^51]
Óðinn owns the earls, those who fall in battle,
but Þórr owns the kindred of thralls!’[^52]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0028 ref="hrbl.25"}
::: stanza
‘You’d divide people unequally among the Æsir,
if you possessed very much power!’[^53]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0029 ref="hrbl.26"}
::: stanza
‘Þórr has sufficient strength, but no heart;
out of dread and cowardice you squeezed into a glove,
and you didn’t seem to be Þórr then;[^54]
because of your dread you then dared neither
sneeze nor fart so that Fjalarr heard!’[^55]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0030 ref="hrbl.27"}
::: stanza
‘Hárbarðr, you pervert, I’d strike you into Hel,[^56]
if I could stretch across the sound!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0031 ref="hrbl.28"}
::: stanza
‘Why should you stretch across the sound, when our disputes are all as none?
What were you doing then, Þórr?’[^57]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0032 ref="hrbl.29"}
::: stanza
‘I was in the east and I defended the river
when Svárangr’s sons assaulted me;[^58]
they pelted me with rocks, but took little pleasure in the victory,[^59]
they first had to beg me for peace\!
What were you doing then, meanwhile, Hárbarðr?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0033 ref="hrbl.30"}
::: stanza
‘I was in the east and I spoke to some woman;[^60]
I played with the linen-white lady and had secret liaisons,[^61]
I gladdened the gold-bright woman, the girl gave me pleasure!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0034 ref="hrbl.31"}
::: stanza
‘You had good dealings with the girl there, then!’[^62]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0035 ref="hrbl.32"}
::: stanza
‘I’d have needed your help then, Þórr,
so that I might hold the linen-white maid!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0036 ref="hrbl.33"}
::: stanza
‘I’d have granted it to you then, if I’d been able!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0037 ref="hrbl.34"}
::: stanza
‘I’d have trusted you then, unless you betrayed me!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0038 ref="hrbl.35"}
::: stanza
‘I’m not such a heel-biter as an old hide shoe in spring!’[^63]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0039 ref="hrbl.36"}
::: stanza
‘What were you doing meanwhile, Þórr?’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0040 ref="hrbl.37"}
::: stanza
‘I was fighting brides of berserks[^64] on Hlésey;[^65]
they’d done the worst things, deceived all humankind!’[^66]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0041 ref="hrbl.38"}
::: stanza
‘You did a shameful thing then, Þórr, when you fought with women!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0042 ref="hrbl.39"}
::: stanza
‘They were she-wolves, and scarcely women;
they smashed my ship, which I’d shored up,
threatened me with iron clubs, and chased Þjálfi\![^67]
What were you doing meanwhile, Hárbarðr?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0043 ref="hrbl.40"}
::: stanza
‘I was in the army which[^68] made ready here
to raise the battle-standard, to redden the spear!’[^69]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0044 ref="hrbl.41"}
::: stanza
‘Now you want to talk about this, when you went to offer us unpleasant terms!’[^70]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0045 ref="hrbl.42"}
::: stanza
‘I shall compensate you for that, then, with an arm-ring,[^71]
just as the arbiters awarded, those who want to reconcile us!’[^72]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0046 ref="hrbl.43"}
::: stanza
‘Where did you learn these offensive words,
than which I’ve never heard more offensive!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0047 ref="hrbl.44"}
::: stanza
‘I learnt them from those old people who live in the forests of home!’[^73]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0048 ref="hrbl.45"}
::: stanza
‘But you’re giving cairns a good[^74] name when you call them “forests of home”!’[^75]
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0049 ref="hrbl.46"}
::: stanza
‘That’s how I think of such things!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0050 ref="hrbl.47"}
::: stanza
‘Your glibness will serve you badly,
if I decide to wade across the bay;
louder than a wolf I think you’ll howl,
if you take a hit from my hammer!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0051 ref="hrbl.48"}
::: stanza
‘Sif has a lover at home,[^76] him you’ll want to meet;
you’ll use your might on him, that’s more urgent for you!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0052 ref="hrbl.49"}
::: stanza
‘You’re shooting your mouth off,[^77] so that it should seem worst to me;
you gutless man, I think you’re lying!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0053 ref="hrbl.50"}
::: stanza
‘I think I speak true, you’re slow on your journey;
you’d have come a long way by now, Þórr, if you’d travelled with oars(?)!’[^78]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0054 ref="hrbl.51"}
::: stanza
‘Hárbarðr, you pervert, it’s rather you who’s delayed me now!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0055 ref="hrbl.52"}
::: stanza
‘I never thought a herdsman[^79] would
thwart the travels of Ása-Þórr!’[^80]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0056 ref="hrbl.53"}
::: stanza
‘I’ll tell you what to do now: row the boat over here\!
Let’s stop bickering — meet Magni’s father!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0057 ref="hrbl.54"}
::: stanza
‘Clear off from the sound! Passage shall be refused you!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0058 ref="hrbl.55"}
::: stanza
‘Show me the way now, since you won’t ferry me over the bay!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0059 ref="hrbl.56"}
::: stanza
‘It’s a little thing to refuse, \[but\] it’s a long way to go:
it’s one stretch to the stump, another to the stone,
then take the left-hand road until you reach Verland;[^81]
there Fjǫrgyn will find Þórr, her son,
and she will show him the kinsmen’s ways to Óðinn’s lands!’[^82]
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0060 ref="hrbl.57"}
::: stanza
‘Will I get there today?’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0061 ref="hrbl.58"}
::: stanza
‘You’ll get there with toil and trouble while the sun’s up,[^83]
as I think it will thaw!’
Þórr said:
:::

{#ch06-0062 ref="hrbl.59"}
::: stanza
‘Short will be our talk now, since you answer me only with scorn;
I’ll pay you back for refusing me passage, if we meet another time!’
Hárbarðr said:
:::

{#ch06-0063 ref="hrbl.60"}
::: stanza
‘Go now, to where fierce ones[^84] may have you whole!’[^85]
:::

[^9]: I.e., from {_Jǫtunheim(a)r_} ‘Giant Home(s)’, the lands in the east where he slays giants. Cf. {_Ls._} initial prose.

[^10]: The ferryman is Óðinn. His identity is half-revealed in {_Hrbl._} 10, but, if the poem were dramatized, may have been more apparent to those watching.

[^11]: A superlative; cf. {_Hdl._} 1, {_Gðr._} 1.

[^12]: Þórr perhaps used {_sveinn_} ‘boy, young man’ in the technical sense ‘servant with a particular function’, {_sveinn sveina_} amounting to a superlative ‘best of servants’. But the ferryman takes this address as an insult to his age and status. Hence the ferryman’s barbed rejoinder: ON {_karl_} denotes an old man of low status. The irony of this exchange is not just that Þórr fails to recognize his father or that the two gods misdescribe each other’s ages, but that each describes the other in terms more appropriate to himself, Þórr sometimes being likened to a young man or boy in Norse myths, and Óðinn often appearing as an old man ({_karl_}).

[^13]: This statement might indicate that night is approaching. Cf. {_Hrbl._} 58.

[^14]: According to the initial prose, Þórr is returning from the enemy territory of Jǫtunheim(a)r, so perhaps he ate enough for a round-trip before setting out from home. Cf. {_Ls._} note to initial prose, {_Ls._} 55, {_Hym._} 15, {_Þrk._} 24.

[^15]: Or ‘and porridge’. Þórr often travels in a chariot drawn by billy-goats. In {_SnEGylf_} (44, p. 37) he eats his goats for supper, before resurrecting them by consecrating their bones with his hammer. It might be this rite that Óðinn refers to as Þórr’s ‘early deeds’ in the next stanza.

[^16]: The implication may be that a true hero would be thinking of fame, not his stomach.

[^17]: Þórr’s mother is a giantess called {_Jǫrð_} ‘Earth’ or, as in {_Hrbl._} 56, {_Fjǫrgyn_} ‘Earth/Mountain’. The ferryman probably alludes to the water ahead—solid earth is no more. He might also allude to winter, when the earth seems dead and many of its inhabitants (‘Þórr’s household’?) are dejected; cf. the imminent thaw mentioned in {_Hrbl._} 58.

[^18]: An insult, as Þórr was a god of farmers and especially popular in Iceland.

[^19]: Cf\. {_Alv._} 5.

[^20]: {_SnESkáld_} (I, 75, p. 113) names {_Hildólfr_} ‘Battle-Wolf’ immediately after Þórr as one of Óðinn’s sons.

[^21]: ‘Counsel-Island Sound’; the name is otherwise unknown but appropriate for the site of a battle of wits.

[^22]: This request is, of course, merely part of Óðinn’s act. He would have recognized Þórr instantly.

[^23]: Meili is another of Óðinn’s sons. He is also named in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 75, p. 113) and in {_Haust._} 4, 14.

[^24]: {_Magni_} ‘Strong One’ is known for his eventual possession, along with his brother Móði, of Þórr’s hammer ({_Vm._} 51); also for the story in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 17, p. 22) of how, aged only three, he alone was strong enough to lift the giant Hrungnir’s leg from the neck of his prostrate father.

[^25]: Ironically, {_Hárbarðr_} ‘Grey Beard’ is an alias of Óðinn, who habitually conceals his identity. In {_Grm._} 49 it is one of his names ‘among the gods’.

[^26]: The second and third verses of this stanza should perhaps be reversed for alliteration.

[^27]: The meaning of {_ǫgur(r)_} is uncertain here; perhaps ‘testicles’, ‘balls’.

[^28]: Cf\. {_SnEGylf_} (45, p. 39), where the giant Skrýmir warns Þórr and his companions that the giant Útgarða-Loki’s men will not tolerate {_þvílíkum kǫgursveinum kǫpuryrði_} ‘cheek from babies like you’. The meaning of {_kǫgur-_} in {_kǫgursveinn_} is uncertain (perhaps ‘swaddling cloth’), but the compound is evidently a derogatory term for a small man.

[^29]: It is curious that, despite his rage, Þórr does not simply wade across the sound here or in {_Hrbl._} 47. By contrast, he wades rivers daily in {_Grm._} 29 and crosses the rivers called Élivágar in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 17, p. 22); furthermore, the account in {_SnEGylf_} (48, pp. 44–45) of how his feet touched the seabed when he was fishing suggests that he ought easily to have waded across a sound. On the other hand, {_SnESkáld_} (I, 18, p. 25) records that he got into severe difficulty when wading the river Vimur, which was being swollen by the urine or menstrual blood of a giantess.

[^30]: Þórr’s slaying of the stone-headed giant Hrungnir is described in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 17, pp. 20–24), according to which he only narrowly survived.

[^31]: Challenge questions are conventional in Norse flytings.

[^32]: The events in this stanza are otherwise unknown, as are {_Fjǫlvarr_} ‘Very Wary’ and {_Algrœn_} ‘All Green’. The former might, however, be a giant, the male counterpart of a giantess called {_Fjǫlvǫr_}. If so, Óðinn’s fighting beside a giant would be unique among surviving myths and perhaps calculated to anger the giant-killer Þórr.

[^33]: Literally, ‘if they became compliant \[or ‘wise’\] to us’.

[^34]: This stanza appears intentionally obscure, and partly nonsensical, in order to puzzle Þórr.

[^35]: I.e., ‘overcame them’. Sexual innuendo is possible.

[^36]: This myth is otherwise unknown and the identity of the seven sisters is unclear. Óðinn might have invented it, and them, though he is famous for sneaky liaisons with women and giantesses.

[^37]: {_Haust._} and {_SnESkáld_} (I, G56, pp. 1–2) record how the giant Þjazi kidnapped the goddess Iðunn, but was eventually killed by the gods. Neither account attributes the killing to Þórr alone; note also {_Ls._} 50.

[^38]: Þjazi. His father {_Allvaldi_} ‘All Ruler’ is called {_Ǫlvaldi_} ‘Ale Ruler’ in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 56, p. 3).

[^39]: In {_SnESkáld_} (I, 56, p. 2) it is Óðinn who throws Þjazi’s eyes into the sky, making them into stars as part of a compensation package for Skaði, the giant’s daughter. It is unclear who does this in a verse from the early skaldic composition {_Ragnarsdrápa_} ‘Ragnarr’s Poem’, quoted in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 23, p. 34 and p. 180 n.). Cf. {_SnESkáld_} (I, 17, p. 22), where Þórr throws one of Aurvandill’s toes into the sky and makes it a star.

[^40]: I.e., cunning ways to seduce young women.

[^41]: Witches\. Cf. {_kveldriða_} ‘evening rider’, a term used of wolf-riding witches and those who brutally ‘ride’ men in the evening.

[^42]: Perhaps ‘their men’. This event is otherwise unknown.

[^43]: {_Hlébarðr_} ‘Leopard’ or ‘Lee Beard’ is otherwise unknown.

[^44]: Cf\. {_FSk._} 32 and note thereto. If the ‘tribute(?)-twig’ is here a metaphor for a sunbeam, its touch perhaps inflicted sunstroke on the giant.

[^45]: Chapter 7 of {_Ynglinga saga_} reports that Óðinn knew how to take away men’s {_vit_} ‘wits’.

[^46]: Sense uncertain, but probably proverbial. Perhaps understand ‘One oak keeps its bark, when another has its scraped off’, or, in other words, ‘Some win, some lose—that’s life!’ Men are often described metaphorically as trees in skaldic poetry.

[^47]: Or ‘rock’, ‘cliff’. Giantesses, like their menfolk, live in rocks. Þórr’s killing of them is celebrated in a skaldic verse in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 4, p. 17), which also recounts his slaying of the daughters of the giant Geirrǫðr (18, pp. 24–30). Þórr kills an old giantess in {_Þrk._} 32. He also claims to have killed two giantesses in chapter 59 of the Icelandic translation of Oddr Snorrason’s late-twelfth-century account of the life of Óláfr Tryggvason.

[^48]: {_Miðgarðr_} ‘Middle Yard/Enclosed Space’, often a term for the world of humans, seems here to refer to a protective wall surrounding that world.

[^49]: Either Gaul or a fictional ‘Land of the Slain ({_valr_})’.

[^50]: Or ‘pursued’.

[^51]: For Óðinn’s incitement of discord, cf. {_HH. II_} 34.

[^52]: Other sources record that Óðinn’s valkyries pick the best noblemen who die in battle. They become the {_einherjar_}, the ‘unique/only champions’ who will fight beside him at Ragnarok. Þórr is usually associated with free farmers, rather than slaves, so this claim looks like another insult.

[^53]: The interpretation of this line is uncertain. An alternative might be ‘if you possessed as much power as you want’.

[^54]: Or ‘and you didn’t think yourself Þórr then’.

[^55]: According to {_SnEGylf_} (45, pp. 37–38), Þórr and his companions once mistook a wide opening for the entrance to a building in which to spend the night. At midnight they heard thunderous noises, felt the ground shake and sought shelter further inside. His companions were afraid, but Þórr grasped his hammer, ready to defend himself. In the morning he realized his mistake—their lodging had been the thumb of a giant’s glove, and the noises and earthquake had been the giant’s snoring. The giant is called {_Skrýmir_} at this stage in {_SnEGylf_} (cf. {_Ls._} 62), but is later revealed as {_Útgarða-Loki_}; the {_Hrbl._} poet apparently knew him by a third name, {_Fjalarr_} (cf. {_fela_} ‘to conceal’). Cf. {_Ls._} 60 and 62, and the glove of the giant Grendel in {_Beowulf_}.

[^56]: The subterranean world of the dead. Cf. {_Ls._} 63.

[^57]: Some lines detailing one of Hárbarðr’s exploits have perhaps been lost before this question.

[^58]: This episode is otherwise unknown, but the sons of {_Svárangr_} ‘Clumsy’ are probably giants, as {_Svárangr_} appears among a list of giant-names in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 75, p. 111; see also p. 172 n. to verse 75/2 therein). The river might be the Ífing, which divides the giants’ territory from that of the gods in {_Vm._} 16; alternatively, it might be one of the Élivágar, which seem to have the same function in {_Hym._} 5.

[^59]: They lost the battle.

[^60]: Probably a giantess. {*A*}’s {_einhæriv_}, by contrast, is the acc. sg. of \*{_einherja_}, an unattested fem. form of {_einheri_}, a term generally used in the pl. to describe undead male champions in Valhǫll; perhaps {*A*} uses the fem. form to refer to a valkyrie.

[^61]: Or, taking {*R*}’s reading, ‘long liaisons’.

[^62]: This line presents textual and interpretative uncertainties.

[^63]: A proverbial statement referring to the pain caused by wearing a dried-up shoe that has survived the winter.

[^64]: {_Berserkir_}, literally ‘bear shirts’, were ferocious warriors, perhaps associated with Óðinn, who flew into a mad rage in battle.

[^65]: ‘Hlér’s (i.e., Ægir’s) Island’ ({_Hlér_} ‘Sea’ being an alias of the sea-giant Ægir), now the Danish island of {_Læsø_} in the Kattegat.

[^66]: This episode is otherwise unknown.

[^67]: Þórr’s manservant, known for his fast running.

[^68]: Or ‘when it’.

[^69]: {_Hrbl._} 40–43 are hard to interpret. The army might be that of the dead, of which Óðinn was leader; cf. {_Hrbl._} 44.

[^70]: Þórr apparently thinks he recognizes his adversary, but no other account of Óðinn threatening the Æsir is known. Perhaps Þórr mistakes the giantess-loving boatman for the sea-god Njǫrðr, who married a giantess and, as one of the Vanir, fought a war against the Æsir that, according to chapter 4 of {_Ynglinga saga_}, was settled by an exchange of hostages.

[^71]: Literally, ‘a ring of the hand’.

[^72]: Hárbarðr perhaps debases the notion of the ring as a sign of good faith by applying the term to his anus. In so doing, he might imply that Þórr enjoys homosexual activity. This could account for Þórr’s outrage in the next stanza—something that an alternative interpretation, of the ring as a manacle, does less well. Cf. {_Ls._} 12.

[^73]: The phrase ‘forests of home’ or ‘the world’s woods’ is not found elsewhere. Many editors emend {_skógum_} and {_Hrbl._} 45 {_skóga_} ‘forests’ to {_haugum_} and {_hauga_} ‘mounds’, respectively, to alliterate with {_heimis_} and get more obvious sense (‘mounds of home’ is closer to the ‘cairns’ of {_Hrbl._} 45). But neither manuscript offers palaeographical support for this; some other verses in {_Hrbl._} also lack alliteration; and forests, as the mysterious dwellings of outlaws and outsiders, make an apt metaphor for the otherworld of the dead. Hárbarðr is doubtless being cryptic to befuddle Þórr.

[^74]: I.e., flattering.

[^75]: Óðinn learns secret knowledge from the dead in {_Háv._} 157 and {_BDr_}.

[^76]: {_Sif_} ‘(Married) Relation’ is Þórr’s wife. No surviving myth proves Hárbarðr’s claim, but Loki claims to have slept with Sif in {_Ls._} 54, and his cutting of her hair in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 35, p. 41) might suggest intimacy.

[^77]: Literally, ‘You’re speaking according to your mouth’s counsel’.

[^78]: The sense of the final words of this stanza ({_ef þú litum fœrir_}) is uncertain. They are sometimes understood to mean ‘if you had travelled through (all the day’s) colours’, i.e., ‘if you had travelled night and day’. But {_litum_} might also mean ‘with oars(?)’ and represent a sexual insult (‘oar’ = ‘penis’), following on from Óðinn’s claim that Þórr has been cuckolded. A loose but effective translation in modern idiom might be ‘if you’d put your oar in’.

[^79]: Óðinn is apparently posing as a lowly herdsman as well as a ferryman.

[^80]: ‘Þórr of the Æsir’, a grand title.

[^81]: ‘Manland’. {*A*} has {_Valland_} ‘Gaul’ or ‘Land of the Slain’; cf. {_Hrbl._} 24.

[^82]: Óðinn tacitly withdraws his earlier claim, in {_Hrbl._} 4, that Þórr’s mother, here called {_Fjǫrgyn_}, is dead.

[^83]: {*A*}’s reading {_at vpp rænnandi solv_} gives different sense: ‘by dawn’.

[^84]: Presumably fiends of some sort.

[^85]: {*A*}’s variant text means ‘Go now, to where all ({_allir_}) fierce ones may have you!’

